= Report Status Lifecycle & Data Retention Strategy


== Project
UPRM Lost & Found Platform

== Issue
#172

== Author
Javier Ventosa
== Goal

Research and define a clear data retention strategy for lost and found reports, including when and how reports should be archived, deactivated, or permanently deleted.

== Purpose

This research establishes structured lifecycle rules for report management within the UPRM Lost & Found platform.

The objective is to balance usability, privacy protection, and system maintainability by defining time-based transitions between report states.

Without defined retention rules, the system risks accumulating outdated reports, exposing user information unnecessarily, and degrading search quality.

== Key Areas Researched

* Retention duration options (30, 60, 90 days)
* Automatic archiving vs manual closure
* Handling stale or abandoned reports
* Data privacy considerations over time
* Archived visibility policy
* Technical feasibility within current architecture

== Retention Duration Analysis

=== 30-Day Retention

*Pros:* Keeps system current, reduces database growth.  
*Cons:* May be too short for item recovery.

=== 60-Day Retention (Recommended)

*Pros:* Balanced duration, reasonable recovery window.  
*Cons:* Some inactive reports may accumulate.

=== 90-Day Retention

*Pros:* Extended recovery opportunity.  
*Cons:* Greater privacy exposure, larger database size.

== Recommended Retention Rule

Default Active Duration: *60 days*

This duration balances usability and system maintainability.

== Automatic Archiving vs Manual Resolution

=== Automatic Archiving

*Pros:* Reduces administrative overhead.  
*Cons:* May archive relevant reports.

=== Manual Resolution

*Pros:* User-controlled and accurate.  
*Cons:* Users may forget to update status.

=== Recommended Hybrid Model

* Users may manually resolve reports at any time.
* System automatically archives after 60 days of inactivity.

== Proposed Lifecycle Model

Open → Matched → Resolved → Archived → Deleted

=== State Definitions

*Open*:: Active and searchable.  
*Matched*:: Potential match identified.  
*Resolved*:: Item returned or confirmed closed.  
*Archived*:: Inactive but preserved.  
*Deleted*:: Permanently removed.

== Archived Report Visibility

Recommended: Archived reports remain searchable but clearly labeled as "Archived."

== Privacy Considerations

* Automatically archive inactive reports
* After 180 days archived → eligible for permanent deletion
* Limit exposure of personal contact details in archived state

== Time-Based Rules Summary

* Open → Active up to 60 days
* After 60 days inactivity → Archived
* After 180 days archived → Eligible for deletion
* Manual resolution allowed anytime

== Technical Feasibility Assessment

=== Backend

* Add `status` field
* Add `last_updated` timestamp
* Add `archived_at` and `deleted_at` timestamps
* Implement scheduled job (cron) for inactivity checks

=== Frontend

* Display status clearly
* Visually distinguish archived reports
* Optionally disable editing when archived

Integration Complexity: Moderate

== Final Recommendation

60-day active retention + automatic archiving + manual resolution option + searchable archived reports + permanent deletion after 180 days.

This approach controls data growth, improves search relevance, and protects user privacy.